WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.460
The fraud check reads one merchant's profile from Redis about a hundred times a second.

2
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.777
On a sale day, every read is a hit.

3
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:13.556
Each copy in Redis has a time to live.

4
00:00:13.556 --> 00:00:16.307
When that runs out, Redis deletes the key.

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.451
Loading the row again takes 200 milliseconds.

6
00:00:19.451 --> 00:00:25.025
Before any load can finish, a hundred requests ask for the key, and all of them miss.

7
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:31.853
None of them knows about the others, so each one queries the database: the same query, a hundred times.

8
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:38.961
Its pool has 20 connections, so 80 requests wait in line, and every other query waits behind them.

9
00:00:38.961 --> 00:00:40.720
This is a cache stampede.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.555
Single-flight fixes it inside one process.

11
00:00:43.555 --> 00:00:49.056
The first request to miss puts a placeholder for the result, called a future, in a map.

12
00:00:49.056 --> 00:00:51.459
Only this leader queries the database.

13
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:55.395
The other 99 find the future in the map and wait on it.

14
00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:02.300
After 200 milliseconds the leader writes the row back to Redis and completes the future.

15
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.625
All 99 wake up with the same row.

16
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.186
That's one query instead of a hundred.

17
00:01:07.186 --> 00:01:11.896
But the map lives in one process, so twenty pods still send twenty queries.

18
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:15.109
Better still, refresh the key before it expires.

19
00:01:15.109 --> 00:01:19.483
But if every read in the last half second reloads, that's 50 reloads.

20
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:22.405
So each read decides alone, at random.

21
00:01:22.405 --> 00:01:27.809
Next to the row, Redis keeps how long the last load took, 200 milliseconds.

22
00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:28.817
Call it delta.

23
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:35.110
Each read draws a random number, usually about one, and pretends it's that many deltas later.

24
00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.620
Far from the expiry, that falls short.

25
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.108
Near the expiry the odds climb fast.

26
00:01:40.108 --> 00:01:46.133
Usually one read lands past it and refreshes early, while the others keep reading the old row.

27
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:48.663
A hot key's misses arrive together.

28
00:01:48.663 --> 00:01:52.789
Let one request load it, or refresh it just before it expires.
